  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186087868,
      "id": "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44",
      "title": "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "aa4bcb80-f1ad-587e-b511-ad0f8a41ed69",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=6{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-2y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bcff9d61-d180-5a05-bc10-ff1b8e0acef8",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
